## Metadata
Author: William Callander
Copyright: New York World
Date: 1914-08-16
Number: 32
Notes: A 13-wide diamond (one square at top and bottom, a 13-square middle row) with a cross-shaped panel over the centre (shown as _: r4c6, r5c5-c7, r6c4-c8, r7c5-c7, r8c6); the numbers are printed inside the end squares. Headed "FUN'S CROSS-WORD PUZZLES No 32"; the number is printed in the heading, and the intro calls it "the thirty-second in FUN'S series". No clue is printed for 17-18 (ANEW in the solution), so the clue list has 31 entries for 32 words. Credit as printed: "Today's cross-word, the thirty-second in FUN'S series, was sent in by William Callander of Pulaski street, Brooklyn. He writes as follows: 'The solving of your cross-word puzzles has been a source of keen delight not only to myself but to various members of my family and friends; but, hard as some of the puzzles have been to solve, I find it much more of a task to construct one.'" Read by hand from the Milwaukee Sentinel's page (Milwaukee_Sentinel_1914_08_16_82.png), solution from Milwaukee_Sentinel_1914_08_23_76.png, by Claude, 2026-11-16.
Numbering: across-first


